From u9c09694062cb7736 at groups.google.com  Thu Nov 12 19:02:11 2009
From: u9c09694062cb7736 at groups.google.com (Steven McKinney)
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2009 19:02:11 +0000
Subject: RE: [aroma.affymetrix] a question
Message-ID: <P2PL8eOeo_0.pk_-t4R4sVsJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <P2PL8eOeo_0.7hjQlUb1JcUJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
References: <P2PL8eOeo_0.7hjQlUb1JcUJ@aroma-affymetrix.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/aroma-affymetrix/c/P2PL8eOeo_0/m/pk_-t4R4sVsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Jiang,

Briefly, the PM means "perfect match" - the 25-mer sequence of A, T, C and G perfectly matches the compliment to the sequence of interest.
MM means "mismatch" - the 13th nucleotide does not match the compliment of the sequence of interest
(G is switched with C, A is switched with T).

see e.g.
http://www.affymetrix.com/support/technical/technotes/25mer_technote.pdf

Some data preprocessing algorithms such as RMA have versions that only use the
perfect match data.  The issues are the same for the MoEx-1_0-st-v1 chip.

Steven McKinney

